## Bug fixed: negative years not zero-padded in ISO output

### Root cause
`padStart(input, n)` in `/luxon/src/impl/util.js` padded the *whole string* including the minus sign:
```js
if (input.toString().length < n) {
  return ("0".repeat(n) + input).slice(-n);   // "-5".length(2) < 4 -> "00-5"
}
```
So `-5` with `n=4` produced `"00-5"` and `-100` produced `"-100"` (no padding at all, since its length is already 4). `DateTime#toISODate()` (src/datetime.js:1525) formats `"yyyy-MM-dd"` via `toTechFormat` → `Formatter.create(..., {forceSimple:true})` → `formatter.js:118 this.num(n, p)` → `padStart(n, p)`, so the broken padding leaked straight into `toISODate()`/`toISO()`/`toISOWeekDate()`/`toFormat("yyyy")`. The bug is genuinely in util.js (shared helper), not in datetime.js/formatter.js.

### Exact diff (only change I made; /luxon/src/impl/util.js lines 101-109)
```diff
 export function padStart(input, n = 2) {
-  if (input.toString().length < n) {
-    return ("0".repeat(n) + input).slice(-n);
+  const isNeg = input < 0;
+  let padded;
+  if (isNeg) {
+    padded = "-" + ("" + -input).padStart(n, "0");
   } else {
-    return input.toString();
+    padded = ("" + input).padStart(n, "0");
   }
+  return padded;
 }
```
Sign is now emitted separately and the digits are padded to `n`; positive numbers behave exactly as before, and it matches what `Intl.NumberFormat` with `minimumIntegerDigits` does (`-5`→`-05`, `-100`→`-0100`), so the `forceSimple` polyfill and the Intl path agree.

### Coordination with the other agent (util.js)
Re-read the file immediately before and after editing. The other agent's `objToLocalTS` fix (`d.setUTCFullYear(obj.year, obj.month - 1, obj.day)`, line 174) is still present and untouched; my edit is confined to the `padStart` body. `git diff src/impl/util.js` shows exactly those two hunks. I created no files in the repo — all scratch files are in `/tmp/chk/` (`iso.test.js`, `verify.js`, `pad.js`, `fmt.js`, `jest.config.js`, `transformer.js`).

